---
/**
 * Section — Open source: the repository, walked through.
 *
 * It sits directly under "Built in public" and finishes the argument that
 * section opens. That one shows a year of commits landing in the open; this
 * one opens the repository they landed in and lets a visitor watch someone
 * read it — the root listing, the application package, the installer
 * directory, and the first lines of the script the install one-liner pipes into
 * a shell.
 *
 * WHY A WALKTHROUGH AND NOT A CARD. The first version of this section was a
 * card: the repository path, a licence badge, five directory names and a clone
 * line. Everything on it was true and none of it was worth looking at — a
 * reader learns the same from one sentence, and the section had no reason to be
 * a screen tall. The maintainer said so on 2026-08-29. A section whose claim is
 * "you can read this" has to SHOW the reading, which is what the page already
 * does three times over for the product's own features. The demo is built the
 * same way those three are, on the same stage.
 *
 * WHY IT IS NOT A SECOND STAR COUNT. Two sections above already carry the
 * figure — the globe's headline number and the stat row's "31 stars" — so
 * nothing in this section's own copy is a number. The counts inside the window
 * are a different thing: they are part of the page being depicted, in the place
 * GitHub itself puts them, and a repository header without them would be the
 * odd one.
 *
 * THE HEAD IS CENTRED. Every other section sets its headline against a left
 * edge it shares with the thing below it. What is below this one is a single
 * object the width of the whole column with no left edge of its own to share,
 * so a left-set headline would hang off one corner of it (maintainer,
 * 2026-08-29).
 *
 * IT ADDS NO SCROLL BEHAVIOUR, deliberately. Its two neighbours are both scroll
 * traps — the stargazer globe holds the reader for 0.8 of a screen, the install
 * steps for four — and a third pinned section between them would leave the page
 * unable to be scrolled past without three separate stops. This is an ordinary
 * block: one screen, scrolled past at the reader's own pace. `SectionTrack`
 * derives its rail from document order, so inserting this section re-points the
 * marker for every section below it with nothing to update by hand.
 *
 * NOTHING IN THE WINDOW IS INVENTED. Every listing, the description, the
 * topics, the licence, the counts, the tip commit and the eighteen lines of
 * source come from `src/data/repo-map.json`, which `scripts/fetch-repo-map.mjs`
 * reads from GitHub's API and commits. See that script and
 * src/components/github-demo/frames.ts.
 *
 * Widths come from the one Container; no max-width is named here.
 * See docs/layout.md and docs/design.md.
 */
import Container from "@/components/Container.astro";
import SectionTrack from "@/components/SectionTrack.astro";
import { GitHubDemo } from "@/components/github-demo/GitHubDemo";
import { REPO } from "@/lib/install";

/**
 * The two ways in under the button, both real files in the repository.
 *
 * CONTRIBUTING.md opens with "your first PR in five steps"; SECURITY.md is the
 * private route the project asks people to use instead of a public issue. Both
 * are files the repository actually carries rather than GitHub tabs that may be
 * empty — a link here that 404s is worse than no link.
 */
const WAYS_IN = [
  { label: "Contributing guide", href: `${REPO}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md` },
  { label: "Security policy", href: `${REPO}/blob/main/SECURITY.md` },
];
---

<!--
  A BOUNDED SECTION, the shape every section on this page uses: the band's two
  hairlines are this section's boundary rules, held `--section-inset` inside its
  own top and bottom edges. No `.section-rule` anywhere in here — opening with
  one on top of a framed body is what drew two and three lines at a joint before
  the shell was unified. See docs/layout.md § "One boundary, one shape".
-->
<section
  id="open-source"
  aria-labelledby="oss-heading"
  class="section-full-height flex flex-col"
>
  <Container width="content" class="section-inset flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col">
    {/*
      The band. `--fill` makes it take the leftover height so the closing rule
      stands an inset above the section's bottom edge rather than stopping where
      the content stops; `justify-center` splits the surplus evenly, because a
      frame closed at both ends reads as a band only when what it holds sits in
      the middle of it.

      `SectionTrack` lives in HERE and not on the <section>: it measures the
      positioned box it is dropped into, and the box the reader sees as the
      section is this one. `nested`, because this block already sits on the
      content column — a second `content` Container would apply its gutter and
      its max-width again and ride the marker 32px inside the rail.
    */}
    <div
      class="section-bounds section-bounds--fill oss-band flex flex-col justify-center"
    >
      <SectionTrack nested />

      <div class="oss-head">
        {/* The register every section opens in. "Open source" and not "In the
            open" — that is the stargazer section's eyebrow two screens up, and
            two sections should not answer to the same label. */}
        <p class="oss-eyebrow">Open source</p>

        {/*
          Two block spans, not a `<br />`: the break lands at the sentence
          boundary at every width, and a block boundary contributes a separator
          to the accessible name where a `<br />` contributes none.

          The claim carries `--ink`, the invitation `--body` — the same
          two-weight head as the section above it, which is what makes the pair
          read as one argument continued rather than as two openings.
        */}
        <h2 id="oss-heading" class="oss-headline">
          <span class="oss-headline__claim">Read it before you run it.</span>
          <span class="oss-headline__invite">Every line is on GitHub.</span>
        </h2>

        {/*
          No licence, no counts, no directory names. All three are in the window
          below, in the places GitHub itself puts them, and a sentence that says
          them first turns the demo into an illustration of the paragraph
          instead of the thing the section is for.
        */}
        <p class="oss-standfirst">
          This is the repository the install command pulls from. Open it
          yourself — nothing here sits behind a signup.
        </p>

        <div class="oss-actions">
          <a class="oss-cta" href={REPO} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Open the repository
          </a>

          <ul class="oss-ways">
            {
              WAYS_IN.map((way) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={way.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                    {way.label}
                  </a>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/*
        The walkthrough. `client:visible`, not `client:load`: it is most of the
        way down the page, and four beats nobody is looking at are a frame
        budget spent on nothing — the same call the globe two sections up makes.
      */}
      <div class="oss-stage">
        <GitHubDemo client:visible />
      </div>
    </div>
  </Container>
</section>

<style>
  /* --- The head ----------------------------------------------------------
   *
   * Centred — see the note at the top of the file for why this section's head
   * is the one that is. The measure still comes from a `ch` limit rather than a
   * column, so it stays a measure as the type scales with the viewport.
   */
  .oss-head {
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* Sized from the tokens rather than Tailwind's rem utilities: the root font
     size grows with the viewport (layout.css), so a `text-4xl` here would climb
     past the display token and this headline would outgrow every other section
     head on the page. */
  .oss-eyebrow {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-caption-upper);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-caption-upper);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .oss-headline {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
    font-size: var(--text-display-lg);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display-lg);
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .oss-headline__claim,
  .oss-headline__invite {
    display: block;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .oss-headline__claim {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .oss-headline__invite {
    color: var(--body);
  }

  .oss-standfirst {
    margin-top: var(--space-base);
    margin-inline: auto;
    max-inline-size: 62ch;
    color: var(--body);
    font-size: var(--text-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* --- The two actions ---------------------------------------------------
   * One button and two text links. The button is paper on ink — this system's
   * primary action, and the reason it needs no borrowed accent hue — shaped
   * like the hero's pair so the page has one CTA and not two.
   */
  .oss-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .oss-cta {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* 44px, the AAA touch target — the same height the hero's CTAs stand at. */
    min-height: 44px;
    padding-inline: var(--space-lg);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--on-ink);
    font-size: var(--text-body-sm);
    font-weight: 500;
    transition: background-color 150ms ease;
  }

  .oss-cta:hover {
    background: var(--ink-active);
  }

  .oss-cta:focus-visible,
  .oss-ways a:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--ink);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .oss-ways {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-base);
  }

  .oss-ways a {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-body-sm);
    text-decoration: underline;
    /* The offset is what keeps the underline off the descenders; without it a
       muted link at this size reads as struck through rather than underlined. */
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    text-decoration-color: var(--hairline-strong);
    transition:
      color 150ms ease,
      text-decoration-color 150ms ease;
  }

  .oss-ways a:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration-color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* --- The stage ---------------------------------------------------------
   *
   * THE ASPECT IS THE WINDOW'S; THE CEILING IS THE SECTION'S. The box takes the
   * canvas proportion, so on a screen with room to spare the well is exactly
   * the shape of what it holds and no backdrop shows around it. The ceiling is
   * what keeps the section to ONE screen on a shorter window: past it the box
   * stops growing, the window inside is fitted by height instead of by width,
   * and the leftover becomes the painting at its sides — which is what the well
   * is for.
   *
   * `360px` is everything else the section owes at that ceiling, INSIDE the two
   * boundary rules, MEASURED and not estimated: the band's own 48px of padding,
   * the 278px head — eyebrow, two lines of headline, two of standfirst and the
   * action row — and this box's 32px top margin. When one of those changes,
   * re-measure and change THIS number rather than the individual rules, the
   * same arrangement the retired video section used for its `--stage-cap`.
   *
   * It is a snapshot at one window, because the head's own height travels with
   * the root font size and no calculation can see it. The error runs the safe
   * way on everything narrower — a smaller head leaves the section slack — and
   * only a window WIDER than the 2560px it was measured at can push past one
   * screen, which is the same exposure section 6 carries.
   *
   * The inset is subtracted as the token and not as a number, because
   * `--section-inset` is itself a clamp on the viewport height: 63px a side at
   * 900px tall, 80px above roughly 1143px. Folding a constant in would be right
   * at exactly one window size and wrong everywhere else.
   */
  .oss-stage {
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
    aspect-ratio: 1440 / 880;
    /* `--section-height`, not `100svh`: this section IS that height — the
     * window less `2 x --nav-height`, see layout.css § "Section heights" — so a
     * cap measured against the whole window lets the stage grow past the box it
     * has to fit in and pushes the closing rule off the bottom of the screen. */
    max-height: clamp(
      240px,
      calc(var(--section-height) - 360px - 2 * var(--section-inset)),
      780px
    );
  }
</style>
